{
  "dom_id": "step-q159694",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\ge 1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-2)}{\\sqrt{13}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.832)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-P(Z<0.83) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.83) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.83)=0.7967.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-Φ(0.83) \\\\  & =1-0.7967 \\\\  & =0.2033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.2033.\n$$",
